# Plan → build (dsh)

This machine's stated method is: reason with the **planner**, hand the plan to
the **builder**, build, verify. On Claude Code that is two subagents on two
different models. **On dsh it is not.**

Await the Planner's terminal output before starting Builder; never pre-spawn
Builder. Use one Planner and one Builder per round. At most one plan-authorized
L1/FE delegation is allowed; multi-workstream work requires an explicit Team
Leader call.

## What dsh actually gives you

{{PLANNER_ROUTING_NOTE}}

{{BUILDER_ROUTING_NOTE}}

So on dsh the loop is a **role discipline**, not model routing. What dsh does add
is real structural support: plan mode holds the planning phase read-only until
`exit_plan_mode` is approved, and the `subagent` tool can run either phase in an
isolated child context.

1. **Plan pass.** Adopt `agent-planner`; plan mode is the natural fit. Investigate
   read-only — read, search, inspect git state, run non-mutating checks. Produce
   verified current state and a done-condition; the smallest real end-to-end
   slice; explicit non-goals/deferred work; a simpler rejected alternative; exact
   affected surfaces and ordered step → verification actions; earliest
   behavioral/live proof; and stop/replan plus install/rollback risks. Write
   **no** production code.
2. **Review the plan.** If it has a real gap, or a decision only the user can
   make, reject or revise it before building. A conversational "sounds good" approves
   nothing — the plan leaves plan mode through `exit_plan_mode`.
3. **Build pass.** Adopt `agent-builder`. Execute the plan's steps in order. If a
   step is wrong or blocked, say so and adapt — do not silently skip it. On the
   first failed or inconclusive real proof, stop scope expansion and only
   diagnose or retry the same slice.
4. **Verify.** Actually run the tests, build, or behaviour the plan named, and
   report the concrete result. A command that exited 0 has told you it finished,
   not that the work happened — assert on the artifact.
5. **Light audit** (enabled={{WORKFLOW_AUDIT_ENABLED}}). Re-read the plan against
   what was built: acceptance criteria met, evidence consistent with the claims,
   nothing unrelated dragged in, no secrets, no destructive action, no follow-up
   silently dropped. Report a verdict, not a reassurance.
6. **Report.** What changed, how it was verified, the audit verdict, what is left.

Running a phase in a `subagent` child gives it clean context, not a different
model, and the child gets none of the profile unless you paste the mission and
boundaries into its task.

## Looping until a condition holds

`/pb is exactly one pass` and reports incomplete evidence rather than retrying.
The portable form is **`/pbg <task> until: <condition>`** — plan → build →
verify, repeated until the condition actually holds. `/pbg is capped at exactly
three rounds`. A second inconclusive proof or two rounds without measurable
progress is BLOCKED even if artifact labels change. Persistence language cannot
override ambiguity, safety, failed proof, no-progress, or round bounds. dsh also
has a harness-side goal loop, but its terminal language cannot override these
portable bounds.

